export const DATA_69: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-097", num: 97, title: "Đề 097", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Social Classes in America", paragraphs: ["Like most countries, the United States has different social classes, groups of people who share a similar level of income, education, and lifestyle. Sociologists usually divide American society into three broad groups: the upper class, the middle class, and the lower class. Although there is no single official rule for deciding who belongs to which class, most experts agree that income and job type are the two biggest factors. People sometimes move between classes during their lives, but for many families, social class tends to stay fairly stable from one generation to the next.", "The upper class makes up only a small percentage of the population, yet it controls a very large share of the country's wealth. Many people in this class earn money not just from a job but from investments, businesses, or property they own. Members of the upper class often attend expensive private schools and universities, and they frequently have strong social connections that help them find good jobs or business opportunities. Because of these advantages, children born into upper-class families often stay in the upper class as adults.", "The middle class is the largest group in American society, although its size has slowly gotten smaller over the past few decades. Middle-class families usually include teachers, nurses, office workers, and small business owners. They typically own their homes, have some savings, and can afford yearly vacations, although they must still budget carefully. Many middle-class parents hope that a college education will help their children either stay in the middle class or move into the upper class. However, rising costs for housing and education have made this goal more difficult for some families in recent years.", "The lower class includes people with low incomes, limited savings, and less access to quality education or healthcare. Many people in this group work in jobs that pay by the hour, such as retail or cleaning jobs, and they may not receive benefits like health insurance from their employer. Housing costs take up a large part of their income, which makes it harder to save money for emergencies. Children from lower-class families sometimes attend schools with fewer resources, which can make it harder for them to move into a higher class later in life.", "Social class in America is not only about money. It also shapes the way people talk, dress, and spend their free time. Researchers have found that people from different classes often have different attitudes about work, family, and success. For example, some studies suggest that upper-class Americans tend to value independence and personal achievement, while lower-class Americans often place greater importance on cooperation and community support. These cultural differences can sometimes create misunderstandings between people from different backgrounds.", "Despite these divisions, many Americans still believe strongly in the idea of the \"American Dream\" — the belief that anyone can improve their social class through hard work. Statistics show that moving from a lower class to a higher class is possible, but it has become harder in recent decades than it used to be. Many experts continue to study why class mobility varies so much between different regions and communities within the same country."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["American social classes differ mainly in income, but culture and mobility play no meaningful role at all.", "The upper class in America has become smaller every year while the lower class has grown rapidly.", "American society is divided into three broad social classes that differ not only in income and lifestyle but also in culture and the ability to move between classes.", "Most Americans move easily and frequently between social classes during their lifetime."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, besides a regular job, where else do many upper-class people often earn money from?", options: ["Government subsidies and public assistance programs.", "Investments, businesses, or property they own.", "Part-time work at retail stores.", "Inheritance from distant relatives only."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is an example of a typical middle-class job mentioned in the passage?", options: ["Investment banker", "Retail cashier", "Nurse", "Company CEO"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is it hard for lower-class families to save money for emergencies?", options: ["Housing costs take up a large part of their income.", "They spend most of their income on private school tuition.", "They are required to pay high taxes on hourly wages.", "They receive full health insurance but no other benefits."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do some studies suggest about lower-class Americans?", options: ["They tend to value independence and personal achievement above all else.", "They often place greater importance on cooperation and community support.", "They rarely form close relationships within their communities.", "They generally share the same attitudes toward work as upper-class Americans."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of the middle class?", options: ["Owning a home and having some savings.", "Being able to afford yearly vacations.", "Hoping education will help children advance socially.", "Facing no financial impact from rising housing and education costs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"stable\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["wealthy", "steady", "complicated", "temporary"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"harder\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["rarer", "slower", "more difficult", "less common"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["health insurance benefits", "many people in this group", "retail employers", "quality schools"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about moving to a higher social class in America today?", options: ["It has become completely impossible for lower-class families in every region.", "It has become much easier than in the past due to new government programs.", "It depends only on personal effort and is unaffected by region or community.", "It is still possible, but statistics show it has become more difficult than in earlier decades."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Surprising Benefits of Violent Video Games", paragraphs: ["For decades, violent video games have been blamed for a range of social problems, from aggressive behavior in children to a general decline in empathy among young people. Parents' groups and some politicians have periodically called for stricter regulation, pointing to games featuring combat, shooting, or graphic conflict as a likely cause of real-world violence. Yet a growing body of psychological research complicates this popular narrative, suggesting that certain violent games may actually confer measurable cognitive and emotional benefits that are rarely acknowledged in public debate.", "One of the most consistently replicated findings concerns visual attention. Several studies have found that players of fast-paced action games, many of which involve combat, demonstrate improved ability to track multiple moving objects simultaneously and to detect visual targets more quickly than non-players. Researchers attribute this enhancement to the constant need to monitor threats appearing unpredictably across a screen, a demand that appears to sharpen the visual system's efficiency over repeated exposure. Notably, this benefit tends to be strongest in games with high-speed action, regardless of whether the on-screen conflict is framed as violent or merely competitive.", "A second area of interest involves emotional regulation under pressure. Some psychologists argue that violent games, precisely because they simulate high-stakes, threatening scenarios, provide players with repeated low-cost opportunities to practice staying calm and making decisions while under simulated stress. A handful of experiments have reported that frequent players of such games show smaller physiological stress responses when facing new stressful tasks, compared with non-players facing the same tasks. Critics of this research, however, caution that the studies are often small and that the direction of causation remains unclear: it is possible that people who are already calm under pressure are simply drawn to violent games in the first place.", "Violent games have also been studied for their effects on problem-solving. Many popular titles require players to develop strategies, manage limited resources, and adapt quickly when a plan fails, skills that some researchers argue transfer, at least modestly, to non-gaming contexts. One frequently cited study found that participants who played a strategy-based violent game for several weeks showed improved performance on unrelated spatial reasoning tasks, although the size of this improvement was relatively small and did not appear in every follow-up study attempting to replicate the result.", "Despite these findings, researchers are careful to note important limitations. The apparent benefits described above are drawn primarily from studies of short-term cognitive performance conducted under controlled laboratory conditions, and it remains uncertain whether these benefits persist over months or years, or whether they translate into meaningful advantages in daily life. Moreover, several large studies examining long-term social behavior have found no clear link between violent game exposure and reduced empathy, but they have likewise found no evidence of a broad social benefit either — the overall picture, researchers emphasize, is one of modest and highly specific effects rather than dramatic ones in either direction.", "The debate is further complicated by the difficulty of isolating violence itself as the relevant variable. Because violent games are often also fast-paced, competitive, and cognitively demanding in ways that many non-violent games are not, some researchers question whether it is the violent content specifically, or simply these shared structural features, that produces the observed cognitive effects. A few comparative studies have found that non-violent games with similar pacing and complexity produce very similar attention and reaction-time benefits, suggesting that violence itself may be largely incidental to the cognitive advantages researchers have documented.", "Given these uncertainties, most researchers in the field now avoid making sweeping claims in either direction. Rather than concluding that violent games are either harmful or beneficial overall, the current consensus leans toward a more cautious position: certain structural features common in many violent games, such as speed and unpredictability, appear to produce specific, modest cognitive benefits, while claims about broader emotional or social harm remain, at best, only weakly supported by the existing evidence."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Violent video games have been scientifically proven to cause a significant decline in empathy among young players.", "Violent video games should be banned because their cognitive benefits are outweighed by their emotional harms.", "Non-violent games are scientifically proven to be more beneficial for cognitive development than violent ones.", "Research suggests that certain structural features common in violent games produce specific, modest cognitive benefits, while claims of broader social harm remain only weakly supported."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do researchers believe causes improved visual attention in players of fast-paced action games?", options: ["The colorful graphics used in most modern action games.", "The constant need to monitor threats appearing unpredictably across the screen.", "The requirement to read on-screen text quickly during gameplay.", "The social pressure of playing against other people online."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what caution do critics raise about research on emotional regulation and violent games?", options: ["The studies show clear evidence that violent games directly cause increased stress in players.", "The direction of causation is unclear, since calmer people may simply be more drawn to violent games.", "The studies were conducted using extremely large and highly diverse samples of players.", "The physiological stress responses measured in these studies are always exaggerated."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the frequently cited study on strategy-based violent games find?", options: ["Participants showed dramatic and consistently replicated improvements in spatial reasoning across all follow-up studies.", "Participants showed no measurable change in problem-solving ability at all.", "Participants showed improved reasoning skills only in tasks directly related to the game itself.", "Participants showed a relatively small improvement in spatial reasoning tasks that did not appear in every follow-up attempt."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do comparative studies suggest about the role of violence itself in producing cognitive benefits?", options: ["Violence itself may be largely incidental, since non-violent games with similar pacing produce similar benefits.", "Violence is the single most important factor in producing cognitive benefits from gaming.", "Non-violent games produce no cognitive benefits whatsoever compared to violent ones.", "Violent content is necessary for any improvement in reaction time to occur."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a finding or limitation of the research described?", options: ["Cognitive benefits are drawn mainly from short-term laboratory studies.", "It remains uncertain whether cognitive benefits persist over months or years.", "Large studies have found a clear, direct link between violent games and reduced empathy.", "Some benefits may be caused by pacing and complexity rather than violent content."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"enhancement\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["requirement", "improvement", "limitation", "distraction"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"confer\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["discuss", "grant", "remove", "measure"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the true source of the attention benefits observed in violent-game players?", options: ["The benefits are likely caused primarily by structural features like speed and unpredictability rather than violent content itself.", "The benefits are caused entirely by the graphic depiction of combat and conflict on screen.", "The benefits only appear in games that are explicitly marketed as violent.", "The benefits disappear entirely once a game's violent content is removed."], answer: 0 }, { q: "How would the researchers most likely respond to a claim that violent games are clearly beneficial overall?", options: ["They would likely urge caution, noting that documented benefits are specific and modest rather than dramatic or fully established.", "They would likely agree completely, since all studies confirm broad and lasting cognitive advantages.", "They would likely dismiss the claim entirely, insisting that no cognitive benefits have ever been found.", "They would likely argue that only non-violent games can ever produce genuine cognitive advantages."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Job-Hopping: Why Young Workers Are Changing Careers More Than Ever", paragraphs: ["Job tenure among younger workers has declined steadily over the past two decades, a trend that has prompted considerable hand-wringing among employers accustomed to a model of career progression built around long-term loyalty to a single organization. Surveys conducted by labor economists suggest that workers under thirty-five now remain in a given position for a median of roughly two to three years, compared with the four or five years typical of previous generations at the same career stage. While popular commentary frequently attributes this shift to a supposed decline in work ethic or commitment among younger employees, a closer examination of labor market data suggests a considerably more complex set of causes.", "Chief among these is a structural change in how compensation typically progresses within organizations. Internal promotions and annual raises have, in many industries, failed to keep pace with the wage premiums offered to external hires possessing comparable experience. Consequently, an employee who remains loyal to a single employer may find that a colleague who switched companies twice in the same period is earning a substantially higher salary for equivalent work. Economists refer to this phenomenon as wage compression for tenured staff, and it creates a powerful financial incentive to change employers periodically rather than wait for internal advancement that may never materialize on a competitive timeline.", "A second contributing factor concerns the erosion of long-term employer commitments that once accompanied employee loyalty. Pension plans that rewarded decades of service with a single company have largely disappeared from the private sector, replaced by portable retirement accounts that impose no penalty for changing employers. Similarly, the decline of strong internal promotion pipelines — partly a consequence of flatter organizational structures with fewer middle-management positions — means that ambitious employees frequently find that meaningful advancement requires moving externally rather than climbing a ladder that has, in a sense, been shortened.", "Technology has also reshaped the practical mechanics of job searching in ways that lower the transaction costs associated with changing employers. Professional networking platforms allow passive job seekers to be identified and approached by recruiters without actively applying anywhere, while salary transparency tools make it considerably easier for employees to benchmark their compensation against market rates in real time. This increased informational transparency arguably accelerates job-switching independent of any change in underlying attitudes toward loyalty; workers simply become aware, sooner and more precisely, of how their compensation compares with available alternatives.", "Not all industries exhibit this pattern to the same degree, however. Sectors characterized by highly specialized, firm-specific knowledge — certain areas of manufacturing engineering, for example, where expertise is tied closely to proprietary processes — continue to show comparatively longer tenure among younger workers, since skills acquired at one employer transfer less readily to competitors. This variation suggests that job-hopping is less a generational disposition than an economically rational response to the particular incentive structures present within a given industry.", "Employers themselves are not uniformly disadvantaged by this shift, despite frequent complaints about training costs and institutional knowledge loss. Some economists argue that frequent employee turnover, while individually costly to the departing employer, may generate broader diffusion of skills and best practices across an industry, as employees carry knowledge and techniques from one firm to another. Whether this diffusion effect meaningfully offsets the direct costs of turnover for any individual company, however, remains a matter of ongoing empirical debate, and the answer likely varies considerably depending on how easily a given firm's specific practices can be replicated elsewhere.", "Ultimately, the rise in job-hopping among younger workers appears to reflect a rational adaptation to a labor market in which loyalty is no longer reliably rewarded through internal advancement, rather than a simple erosion of commitment or work ethic. Employers hoping to reduce turnover may find that revising compensation structures to reward tenure competitively proves more effective than appeals to loyalty alone, since the underlying behavior being addressed is, for the most part, a response to financial incentives rather than a shift in generational values."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Younger workers change jobs more frequently mainly because they lack loyalty and work ethic compared to previous generations.", "The rise in job-hopping among younger workers largely reflects a rational response to compensation structures and reduced internal advancement, rather than a decline in commitment.", "Job-hopping occurs equally across all industries regardless of how specialized the required skills are.", "Employers benefit financially in every case when employees change jobs frequently."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is \"wage compression for tenured staff\"?", options: ["A policy in which companies reduce salaries for employees who have worked there the longest.", "A situation in which loyal employees earn less than colleagues who switched employers for comparable work.", "A government regulation limiting how much salaries can increase annually.", "A method companies use to calculate retirement benefits for long-term staff."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what change in retirement benefits has contributed to increased job-hopping?", options: ["Pension plans rewarding long service have been replaced by portable retirement accounts with no penalty for switching employers.", "Companies now offer larger pensions to employees who remain with a single employer for decades.", "Portable retirement accounts have been eliminated in favor of traditional pension plans.", "Middle-management positions have increased significantly, creating more promotion opportunities."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how has technology influenced job-switching behavior?", options: ["It has made passive job seekers harder for recruiters to find.", "It has eliminated the need for salary transparency in most industries.", "It has lowered the transaction costs of job searching by increasing informational transparency about compensation.", "It has primarily reduced the number of available job openings across industries."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do certain manufacturing engineering roles show longer employee tenure?", options: ["Because these roles pay significantly less than other industries.", "Because skills acquired are tied closely to proprietary processes and transfer less readily to competitors.", "Because these industries do not offer any retirement benefits at all.", "Because employees in these roles are legally required to remain with one employer."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a genuine cause of increased job-hopping?", options: ["Wage compression that rewards external hires over loyal employees.", "The disappearance of traditional pension plans tied to long service.", "A general decline in work ethic and commitment among younger workers.", "Increased salary transparency enabled by networking and benchmarking tools."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"erosion\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["decline", "creation", "reversal", "expansion"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"diffusion\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["restriction", "spread", "loss", "concentration"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the true nature of job-hopping, based on the variation observed across industries?", options: ["Job-hopping is best explained as an economically rational response to industry-specific incentives rather than a fixed generational trait.", "Job-hopping occurs at identical rates in every industry regardless of skill specialization.", "Job-hopping is caused entirely by personal preferences unrelated to any economic factor.", "Job-hopping will eventually disappear once all industries adopt portable retirement accounts."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What would the author most likely recommend to employers hoping to reduce turnover?", options: ["Focus on appeals to loyalty and company culture rather than adjusting compensation.", "Eliminate portable retirement accounts to discourage employees from leaving.", "Revise compensation structures to competitively reward tenure, since turnover is largely a response to financial incentives.", "Reduce salary transparency tools so employees cannot compare their pay to competitors."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Burning Man: Art, Community, and Survival in the Black Rock Desert", paragraphs: ["Each summer, tens of thousands of participants converge upon a remote stretch of Nevada's Black Rock Desert to construct, inhabit, and ultimately dismantle a temporary city devoted to art, radical self-expression, and communal survival. Burning Man, now in its fourth decade, presents a curious paradox to outside observers: an event that began as a small, informal gathering on a San Francisco beach has evolved into a logistically formidable undertaking involving sophisticated infrastructure, and yet its organizers insist, not entirely convincingly to skeptics, that the event's founding countercultural ethos remains intact despite this scale.", "Central to Burning Man's self-conception is a set of ten guiding principles, formally articulated by the event's founders, which include radical inclusion, gifting, decommodification, and what participants term \"leaving no trace\" — an exacting commitment to removing every trace of human presence from the desert floor once the event concludes. This last principle is not merely aspirational rhetoric; a dedicated cleanup crew spends weeks afterward conducting a systematic sweep for microscopic debris, and the organization has, on occasion, been required to demonstrate compliance with land-use agreements to skeptical federal regulators. [1] Not only does this commitment shape how the event is planned, but it arguably functions as a kind of ongoing performance of environmental accountability, one intended to preempt objections that a temporary city of this scale is inherently at odds with ecological stewardship. [2]", "The principle of decommodification — the prohibition on buying or selling goods within the event, apart from ice and coffee sold at designated outposts — sits in an uneasy relationship with the considerable sums participants spend to attend. Tickets, transportation, elaborate costumes, art installations, and specialized desert survival equipment collectively represent a substantial financial outlay, one that has drawn persistent criticism that the event's anti-commercial ideals mask what is, in practice, an expensive undertaking accessible mainly to those with considerable disposable income. [3] Defenders of the event counter that the absence of commerce within its temporary boundaries, whatever the cost of arriving there, remains a meaningfully distinct achievement, since it removes the transactional logic that governs most large public gatherings once participants are actually present. [4]", "Survival in the Black Rock Desert imposes genuine physical constraints that shape nearly every aspect of the event's culture. Daytime temperatures frequently exceed forty degrees Celsius, punctuated by sudden dust storms capable of reducing visibility to a matter of meters within minutes, while nighttime temperatures can drop sharply enough to require substantial insulation. Because the desert floor, known as the playa, supports no vegetation and offers no natural water source, every participant must arrive fully self-sufficient, transporting sufficient water, food, and shelter for the event's duration — a logistical requirement that organizers argue functions not merely as a practical necessity but as a deliberate filter, ensuring that attendance requires a degree of preparation and resourcefulness that shapes the culture of mutual aid for which the event is known.", "The event's artistic output, meanwhile, has grown considerably more ambitious over the decades, evolving from modest sculptures toward monumental, often mechanically intricate installations funded through a competitive grant process and constructed by volunteer collectives over the course of many months. The culminating ritual burning of the event's namesake wooden effigy, along with a subsequent burning of a temporary temple structure dedicated to collective mourning and reflection, anchors the event's emotional arc; participants frequently describe these ceremonies in terms that suggest quasi-religious significance, notwithstanding the event's formally secular and areligious self-presentation.", "Critics of Burning Man's trajectory argue that its growing prominence among wealthy technology entrepreneurs — some of whom now arrive via private aircraft and stay in elaborately staffed camps offering amenities unavailable to the general population of attendees — represents a fundamental betrayal of the event's founding egalitarian aspirations. Defenders respond that such criticisms, while not without merit, tend to overstate the degree to which any single demographic can be said to define an event whose scale now encompasses an extraordinarily heterogeneous population, and that pointing to the presence of wealthy attendees does not, by itself, establish that the broader participant experience has been meaningfully corrupted.", "Whether Burning Man can indefinitely sustain the tension between countercultural aspiration and logistical, financial, and demographic realities remains an open question that even long-time participants debate without consensus. What seems less contestable is that the event's continued growth has not, so far, forced organizers to abandon its foundational principles outright, even as those principles are subjected to ever more searching scrutiny regarding how fully they are realized in practice at a scale far removed from the event's modest beach-bonfire origins."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Burning Man has completely abandoned its founding countercultural principles due to the influence of wealthy technology entrepreneurs.", "Burning Man's growth has created an unresolved tension between its founding countercultural ideals and the logistical, financial, and demographic realities of a large-scale event, without organizers being forced to abandon its principles outright.", "Burning Man remains a small, informal beach gathering that has changed very little since its founding.", "Burning Man's decommodification principle has completely eliminated all financial barriers to attending the event."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the \"leaving no trace\" principle require of organizers?", options: ["A dedicated crew conducts a systematic sweep for microscopic debris after the event concludes.", "Organizers must relocate the event to a different desert location every year.", "Participants must donate all art installations to a museum after the event.", "Federal regulators are required to personally supervise the entire event each year."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is permitted for sale within the event under the decommodification principle?", options: ["Costumes and specialized survival equipment.", "Tickets and transportation services.", "Art installations created by volunteer collectives.", "Ice and coffee sold at designated outposts."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why must every participant arrive fully self-sufficient?", options: ["Because organizers charge extra fees for water and food distributed on-site.", "Because the playa supports no vegetation and offers no natural water source.", "Because federal regulations prohibit bringing outside supplies into the desert.", "Because nighttime temperatures remain warm enough that no shelter is needed."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what anchors the event's emotional arc?", options: ["The culminating burning of the wooden effigy and the temple structure dedicated to mourning and reflection.", "A competitive grant process that funds art installations throughout the year.", "A formal religious ceremony conducted by the event's founders.", "The construction of monumental sculptures by paid professional artists."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT accurately presented as a feature of the Black Rock Desert environment?", options: ["Daytime temperatures frequently exceed forty degrees Celsius.", "Sudden dust storms can reduce visibility to a matter of meters.", "The playa contains a limited but sufficient natural water source for participants.", "Nighttime temperatures can drop sharply, requiring substantial insulation."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"heterogeneous\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["wealthy", "diverse", "declining", "unified"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"aspirational\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["mandatory", "idealistic", "outdated", "commercial"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's stance on criticism of wealthy attendees at Burning Man?", options: ["The criticism is entirely baseless and reflects no genuine problem with the event.", "The criticism proves conclusively that Burning Man has become an event exclusively for the wealthy.", "The criticism is irrelevant because wealthy attendees make up the majority of participants.", "The criticism has some merit but does not, by itself, prove that the broader participant experience has been fundamentally corrupted."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This apparent contradiction has become one of the most frequently cited criticisms of the event's professed values.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-136", num: 136, title: "Đề 136", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Tran Nguyen: A Painter Who Brings Dreams to Life", paragraphs: ["Tran Nguyen is a Vietnamese-American artist who creates paintings that look like dreams. She is best known for pictures of quiet, thoughtful women who exist in strange, beautiful worlds. Born in the small city of Can Tho in southern Vietnam, Nguyen later grew up in the United States, where she built a career that mixes fine art and illustration. Today, she works from a home studio in Atlanta, Georgia, and her artwork has been shown in galleries on several continents. Many people who look at her paintings say they feel both calm and curious, as if they have stepped into a story without an ending.", "Nguyen's family did not have an easy start in life. She was born in 1987, and her parents had grown up poor, living in a small house with a dirt floor. In 1990, when Nguyen was only three years old, her family moved to a small town in Georgia, in the United States. Her father worked as a cook, and her mother stayed home to take care of the children. Although the family had very little money, they made sure Nguyen could choose her own path. This decision would later shape the direction of her whole career.", "As a child, Nguyen loved watching Japanese cartoons, and these shows gave her a deep interest in visual storytelling. She often sat with a small painting kit her parents had given her and tried to copy the scenes she saw on screen. By the time she reached high school, she already knew that she wanted to study art. She later attended the Savannah College of Art and Design, where she chose to major in illustration rather than fine art. This choice allowed her to focus on creating single, powerful images that could tell a complete story on their own.", "Nguyen's paintings have a soft, delicate look because she uses thin layers of acrylic paint together with colored pencil. Her favorite subject is women who appear realistic at first glance but who exist inside slightly impossible scenes — a birdcage that holds tiny stars instead of birds, for example. She has explained that her goal is to give viewers a feeling of comfort, no matter what difficulties they may be facing in their own lives. Many fans say that her paintings feel personal, as though each one is a quiet message meant just for them.", "Over the years, Nguyen's talent has earned her wide recognition . She has received several awards from major illustration organizations, including honors from the Society of Illustrators. Well-known companies such as VH1, Netflix, and the World Wildlife Fund have hired her to create artwork, and she once worked as a background artist for the animated television series Archer. In addition to her studio paintings, Nguyen has also painted large murals in cities as far apart as Hawaii and China, bringing her dreamlike style to public spaces that thousands of people walk past every day.", "Today, Nguyen continues to balance gallery work, freelance illustration, and mural projects, never settling into just one type of art. Her path from a small house with a dirt floor to international art shows demonstrates how far determination and imagination can carry someone. For young artists who admire her work, Nguyen's story offers a simple but powerful lesson: a single, carefully made image can speak louder than a thousand ordinary ones."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Tran Nguyen turned a difficult childhood into a career built on imaginative, dreamlike art.", "Tran Nguyen's success came mostly from her television work, while her paintings were a smaller part of her career.", "Tran Nguyen believes art should always copy real life exactly.", "The passage focuses mainly on the awards Tran Nguyen has won throughout her career."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, where was Tran Nguyen born?", options: ["Augusta, Georgia", "Atlanta, Georgia", "Can Tho, Vietnam", "Hephzibah, Georgia"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did Nguyen's father do for work after the family moved to the United States?", options: ["He worked as an illustrator.", "He worked as a painter.", "He worked as a teacher.", "He worked as a cook."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which organization is described as having given Tran Nguyen several major awards?", options: ["World Wildlife Fund", "Netflix", "Society of Illustrators", "VH1"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of Tran Nguyen's career or art?", options: ["Directing an animated film", "Painting large murals in public places", "Using colored pencil and acrylic paint", "Studying illustration at an art college"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"delicate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["colorful", "gentle", "careless", "fragile"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"recognition\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["payment", "fame", "sadness", "popularity"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The father's work as a cook", "Nguyen's freedom to choose her own career path", "The family's lack of money", "Nguyen's move to the United States"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the role of Nguyen's childhood experiences in her art?", options: ["Her difficult childhood directly inspired the exact images she paints today.", "Nguyen avoided talking about her childhood in interviews.", "Her early hardship and the freedom her family gave her shaped both her resilience and her artistic path.", "Nguyen's parents were artists who taught her how to paint."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To inform readers about Nguyen's life and inspire admiration for her artistic journey", "To compare Nguyen's painting style with other surrealist artists", "To warn aspiring artists about the financial risks of an art career", "To criticize the lack of support Nguyen received from her family"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Tracing the Origins and Spread of Coffee", paragraphs: ["Coffee is now one of the most widely consumed beverages on the planet, yet the precise origins of the plant's cultivation remain difficult to pin down with certainty. Historians generally agree that the story blends documented trade routes with oral legend, making it nearly impossible to separate verified fact from popular myth. What can be said with more confidence is that coffee's journey from a wild shrub in East Africa to a global commodity took place over several centuries, passing through religious communities, bustling marketplaces, and eventually the salons of European capitals.", "According to a frequently repeated legend, an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi first noticed the effects of coffee sometime before the fifteenth century. His goats, he reportedly observed, became unusually energetic after eating berries from a particular shrub. Curious, Kaldi is said to have sampled the berries himself, after which he brought them to a nearby monastery. The monks, the story goes, experimented with the berries and found that a drink made from them helped them remain alert during long hours of evening prayer. While charming, this tale cannot be verified, and most historians treat it as folklore rather than documented history.", "What is better documented is the cultivation of coffee in Yemen by the fifteenth century, where Sufi religious communities are believed to have grown and brewed it to support nighttime devotional practices. The drink's stimulating properties made it especially useful for monks who needed to stay awake during extended sessions of prayer and chanting. From Yemen, coffee cultivation and trade expanded outward through the Arabian port city of Mocha, a name that would later become permanently associated with the beverage itself. By the sixteenth century, coffee had become deeply embedded in religious and social life across the Arabian Peninsula, no longer confined to monastic settings alone.", "As coffee moved northward into the Ottoman Empire, it gave rise to a new kind of public institution: the coffeehouse. In cities such as Istanbul, these venues quickly became spaces where men gathered not only to drink coffee but also to play games, discuss current events, and occasionally voice political opinions. This last function made some authorities distinctly uneasy. At various points, Ottoman officials attempted to close coffeehouses altogether, fearing that the free exchange of ideas occurring within them might encourage dissent. Despite these periodic crackdowns, the institution proved remarkably resilient and continued to multiply across the empire's major cities.", "Coffee reached Europe through Venetian merchants engaged in trade with the Ottoman world, arriving sometime in the late sixteenth or early seventeenth century. Its foreign origin initially provoked suspicion among some Christian clergy, who reportedly labeled it a dangerous, almost sinful drink. According to a popular anecdote, Pope Clement VIII was urged to ban the beverage but instead chose to taste it himself; finding it agreeable, he is said to have given it his approval, effectively easing its path into Christian Europe. Whether or not this story is entirely accurate, coffeehouses began appearing across major European cities soon afterward.", "Once established in Europe, coffeehouses evolved into something far more significant than simple refreshment stops. In London, they earned the nickname \"penny universities,\" since the price of a single cup bought admission to hours of conversation, debate, and the exchange of ideas among scientists, writers, and merchants. Some of these gathering places eventually developed into formal institutions; the insurance market known today as Lloyd's of London, for instance, is widely understood to have originated in a seventeenth-century coffeehouse frequented by shipowners and merchants. Historians often credit these informal meeting spaces with playing a meaningful, if indirect, role in the intellectual ferment of the Enlightenment.", "The final stage of coffee's spread involved cultivation rather than consumption. Eager to reduce their dependence on Arabian exports, European colonial powers transplanted coffee seedlings to their overseas territories during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. The Dutch established plantations in Java, while the French introduced the crop to the Caribbean, from which cultivation eventually spread throughout much of Latin America. This shift transformed coffee from a regional specialty into a global commodity produced largely in the tropics yet consumed overwhelmingly in temperate, industrialized regions — a pattern of production and consumption that continues to define the coffee trade today."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Coffee cultivation has remained essentially unchanged since its discovery in Ethiopia.", "Coffee's popularity is mainly the result of a single discovery by Ethiopian monks.", "European coffeehouses were responsible for most of the political unrest in the Ottoman Empire.", "Coffee spread gradually from a regional plant into a global commodity through religious, commercial, and political channels."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is Kaldi traditionally said to have done after noticing his goats' behavior?", options: ["He immediately built a monastery.", "He sold the berries at a nearby market.", "He reported the goats to local authorities.", "He tried the berries himself before bringing them to a monastery."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did Sufi communities in Yemen value coffee?", options: ["It allowed traders to move goods more efficiently through the port of Mocha.", "It was used as currency in local markets.", "It cured common illnesses among monks.", "It helped them stay awake during nighttime prayer and chanting."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did some Ottoman authorities attempt to close coffeehouses?", options: ["They believed coffeehouses encouraged dissent through free discussion of ideas.", "They believed coffee was a sacred drink reserved for clergy only.", "They believed coffeehouses were the origin of the institution known as Lloyd's of London.", "They wanted to protect local tea merchants from competition."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a place where coffee cultivation or coffeehouses became established?", options: ["London", "Java", "Istanbul", "Brazil"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"resilient\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["profitable", "flexible", "enduring", "fragile"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"ferment\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["decline", "trade", "excitement", "agreement"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This shift\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["The transplanting of coffee cultivation from Arabia to European colonies", "The invention of new methods for brewing coffee", "The decline of Arabian coffee exports", "The growing global demand for coffee"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the relationship between coffeehouses and political authority?", options: ["Both Ottoman and European authorities consistently banned coffeehouses to prevent any public gathering.", "Ottoman officials closed coffeehouses primarily to protect local tea merchants.", "Coffeehouses sometimes provoked unease among those in power because they functioned as informal centers of discussion and exchange.", "Pope Clement VIII personally tasted coffee before deciding whether to approve it."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's primary purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To trace how coffee evolved from a regional plant into a global commodity through religious, commercial, and political history.", "To criticize European colonial powers for exploiting coffee-growing regions.", "To express strong admiration for the taste and quality of different coffee varieties.", "To argue that coffeehouses were the main cause of the Enlightenment."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Reassessing the Origins of the Industrial Revolution", paragraphs: ["The Industrial Revolution has traditionally been narrated as a distinctly British achievement, one made possible by a fortunate convergence of coal, capital, and culture. In recent decades, however, this narrative has come under sustained scrutiny from economic historians who argue that the standard account oversimplifies a far more contingent and globally entangled process. Rather than treating Britain's transformation as the inevitable culmination of European cultural or intellectual superiority, revisionist scholars increasingly situate it within a comparative framework that asks why divergence occurred when and where it did, rather than assuming it was destined to happen at all.", "The conventional account, which dominated historical writing well into the twentieth century, emphasized factors thought to be unique to northwestern Europe: a Protestant ethic conducive to thrift and labor discipline, a scientific tradition stretching back to the Enlightenment, and political institutions that had gradually curtailed royal authority. Coal reserves and a sprawling colonial empire were typically folded into this story as supporting, rather than central, elements. Such explanations implicitly framed industrialization as an outgrowth of cultural attributes that other regions, by implication, simply lacked.", "This framing was significantly destabilized by Kenneth Pomeranz's influential comparison of England with the Yangtze Delta, one of the most economically advanced regions of Qing China. Pomeranz demonstrated that, as late as the mid-eighteenth century, living standards, market sophistication, and proto-industrial output in the Yangtze Delta were broadly comparable to those of England, casting doubt on the assumption that Europe had already pulled decisively ahead before industrialization began. If two regions were economically similar on the eve of divergence, he argued, then cultural explanations alone could not account for why one underwent rapid mechanization while the other did not.", "Pomeranz instead pointed to a combination of geographic accident and imperial extraction. Britain's coal deposits happened to be located close to centers of manufacturing and transportable by water, whereas China's coal reserves lay at a considerable distance from the Yangtze Delta's commercial core. Compounding this advantage, British access to New World land — what some historians term 'ghost acreage' — allowed the colonies to supply land-intensive goods such as cotton and sugar, freeing domestic land in Britain for other uses. China, lacking a comparable overseas hinterland, faced mounting ecological constraints that the Yangtze Delta could not easily resolve through trade.", "A separate strand of scholarship locates the roots of industrialization not in resources but in institutions. Proponents of this view argue that the political settlement following England's Glorious Revolution of 1688 gradually constrained arbitrary taxation and strengthened property rights, thereby giving inventors and merchants greater confidence that the returns on innovation would not be expropriated. Patent law, however imperfect, offered a further incentive structure largely absent elsewhere. Critics of this institutional account counter that similar legal protections existed in other commercially sophisticated societies without producing comparable industrial breakthroughs, suggesting that institutions alone cannot fully explain the timing of British industrialization.", "A third explanatory thread, associated with the historian Jan de Vries, emphasizes shifts in household behavior that preceded mechanized production. According to the so-called industrious revolution thesis, European households began working longer hours and reallocating labor toward market-oriented production well before the steam engine appeared, driven by a growing appetite for manufactured goods such as textiles, ceramics, and printed material. This rising consumer demand, de Vries suggests, created the market conditions that later made large-scale mechanized production commercially viable, effectively reversing the causal arrow that earlier accounts had assumed ran exclusively from supply-side innovation to consumption.", "None of these explanations operates entirely in isolation, and most contemporary historians now resist attributing industrialization to any single cause. Instead, a growing consensus treats coal geography, colonial extraction, institutional change, and shifting consumption patterns as interacting and mutually reinforcing conditions whose precise combination was, to a meaningful degree, historically contingent rather than predetermined. This shift in interpretation carries implications well beyond academic history, since explanations that once implied an inherent civilizational advantage for Europe are increasingly viewed as artifacts of a particular, geographically uneven set of circumstances that might plausibly have unfolded elsewhere under different conditions."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Economic historians agree that Chinese regions were always less developed than Britain before industrialization.", "Institutional reforms following 1688 were universally accepted as the primary cause of industrialization.", "Britain's access to New World land allowed it to industrialize before any other region in the world.", "Historians now favor a multi-causal explanation for British industrialization that questions earlier assumptions of inherent European superiority."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did Pomeranz's comparison between England and the Yangtze Delta reveal about the period before industrialization?", options: ["The Yangtze Delta's living standards and market development were broadly similar to England's.", "The Yangtze Delta's coal reserves were located far from its economic core.", "England had no comparable trading networks to the Yangtze Delta.", "The Yangtze Delta lacked any form of proto-industrial production."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what geographic factor is cited as an advantage for Britain's coal supply?", options: ["Britain's coal deposits were located near manufacturing centers and could be transported by water.", "Britain's New World colonies contained extensive coal reserves of their own.", "Britain's coal deposits were of significantly higher chemical quality than China's.", "Britain imported all of its coal from continental Europe."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the institutional explanation for industrialization differ from the industrious revolution thesis?", options: ["The institutional explanation focuses on consumer demand, while the industrious revolution thesis focuses on coal geography.", "The institutional explanation emphasizes property rights and incentives for innovation, while the industrious revolution thesis emphasizes household labor and rising consumer demand before mechanization.", "Both explanations agree that mechanized production preceded any change in household behavior.", "The institutional explanation argues that patent law existed only in China, not in Britain."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a factor historians have proposed to explain British industrialization?", options: ["Household-level shifts toward greater market-oriented production before mechanization", "The complete absence of coal reserves anywhere in China", "Institutional changes following the Glorious Revolution that strengthened property rights", "The conclusion that European cultural superiority was the primary driver of industrialization"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"destabilized\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["financed", "undermined", "clarified", "reinforced"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"contingent\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["profitable", "accidental", "inevitable", "dependent"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"This shift\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["The replacement of institutional theories with consumer demand theories", "The change from single-cause explanations of industrialization to a multi-causal, contingent framework", "The decline of coal as an explanatory factor", "The discovery of new historical documents about the Glorious Revolution"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the implications of Pomeranz's comparative approach for understanding global economic history?", options: ["Pomeranz's approach proves that China would have industrialized first if it had access to British coal reserves.", "Pomeranz's approach was rejected by most historians shortly after publication.", "Pomeranz's approach shows that institutional differences were ultimately irrelevant to industrialization.", "Pomeranz's approach suggests that regions can appear economically similar shortly before diverging sharply, meaning divergence requires explanation rather than being assumed as a natural outcome of cultural difference."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's primary purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To advocate for adopting de Vries's industrious revolution thesis over all other explanations.", "To criticize Kenneth Pomeranz's methodology in comparative economic history.", "To argue that cultural explanations for industrialization are entirely without merit.", "To present and weigh competing scholarly explanations for why industrialization began where and when it did."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Beyond the Classroom: The Contested Rise of Homeschooling", paragraphs: ["Once a marginal alternative associated almost exclusively with religious or countercultural communities, homeschooling has, over the past two decades, evolved into a mainstream educational option practiced by families across a far wider ideological and socioeconomic spectrum. Its rapid expansion, particularly the surge recorded during pandemic-era school closures, has thrust the practice into renewed public debate, prompting policymakers, researchers, and parents alike to reconsider long-held assumptions about what constitutes an adequate education. Whether this growth represents a durable shift in educational preference or a temporary departure born of crisis circumstances remains, at this stage, genuinely unresolved.", "The motivations driving families toward homeschooling are rarely singular. Surveys conducted across multiple jurisdictions suggest that concerns about school safety, dissatisfaction with curricular content, and a desire to tailor instructional pace to an individual child's needs each contribute meaningfully, though their relative weight varies considerably by region and demographic group. Religious or values-based considerations, once assumed to dominate parental decision-making, now appear to constitute only one motivation among several rather than the primary driver many earlier accounts presumed them to be.", "Advocates frequently point to research suggesting that homeschooled students perform at or above grade level on standardized assessments and report comparatively high rates of subsequent college enrollment. Such findings, however, arguably say less about homeschooling's inherent pedagogical superiority than about who chooses to homeschool in the first place. Families who select this path and subsequently participate in formal research tend, on average, to possess greater financial resources, higher levels of parental education, and more flexible employment arrangements than the general population — characteristics independently associated with stronger academic outcomes regardless of schooling format.", "This selection effect complicates any straightforward causal claim. Because homeschooling families are rarely assigned to that arrangement at random, and because those who decline to participate in surveys may differ systematically from those who do, researchers cannot easily disentangle the effect of the educational method itself from the effect of the preexisting advantages that often accompany the decision to pursue it. Consequently, while the available data does not contradict claims of comparable or superior academic performance, it does not, on its own, establish that homeschooling causes such outcomes.", "Critics raise a related but distinct concern regarding socialization, arguing that children educated outside conventional school settings may have fewer opportunities to interact with peers from differing backgrounds and perspectives. This concern does not necessarily imply that homeschooled children are socially isolated in any absolute sense; many participate in cooperative learning groups, community sports leagues, and extracurricular programs that provide regular peer contact. Rather, the more defensible version of the critique suggests that such interactions, because they are often deliberately curated by parents, may expose children to a narrower range of viewpoints than the comparatively unfiltered social environment of a conventional classroom.", "Regulatory approaches to homeschooling vary considerably both within and across countries, ranging from jurisdictions that impose virtually no oversight beyond an initial notification to those that mandate standardized testing, curriculum approval, or periodic portfolio review. This patchwork reflects an underlying and largely unresolved tension between two legitimate but competing interests: a parent's recognized authority to direct a child's upbringing and education, and the state's countervailing interest in ensuring that every child, regardless of educational setting, receives instruction sufficient to participate meaningfully in civic and economic life.", "Some of the most forceful arguments for stronger oversight draw on documented cases in which the absence of monitoring allowed educational neglect, or in rarer instances abuse, to go undetected for extended periods. Advocacy organizations supporting expanded regulation argue that even infrequent occurrences justify systematic safeguards, given the severity of potential harm. Homeschooling advocates, while rarely disputing the seriousness of such cases, caution against treating them as representative, arguing that policy built primarily around worst-case scenarios risks imposing burdensome requirements on the considerable majority of families for whom no such concerns exist.", "Ultimately, the homeschooling debate may be less a disagreement over educational outcomes than a deeper, largely unresolved dispute about where authority over a child's education should properly reside — within the family or within the state. Because this disagreement is rooted in differing value commitments rather than in any single empirical question that further research could conclusively settle, it seems unlikely that the debate will be resolved soon, even as the practice itself continues to expand into an increasingly diverse range of communities."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Government regulation of homeschooling should be increased in every jurisdiction to prevent neglect.", "Most homeschooling families experience significant social isolation as a direct result of their educational choice.", "The debate over homeschooling reflects unresolved tensions between parental authority and state interest, complicated by limited causal evidence on outcomes.", "Homeschooling produces better academic outcomes than conventional schooling."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the research on homeschooled students' standardized test performance actually establish, according to the author?", options: ["It establishes that homeschooling is academically superior to conventional schooling for all students.", "It shows correlation between homeschooling and certain favorable outcomes, but does not establish that homeschooling itself causes those outcomes.", "It conclusively proves that family income has no effect on academic performance.", "It shows that standardized tests are not a valid measure of academic achievement."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one reason critics raise concerns about homeschooled children's socialization?", options: ["They may be exposed to a narrower range of viewpoints because peer interactions are often selected by parents.", "They are, in nearly all documented cases, unable to participate in any group activities outside the home.", "They are legally prohibited from joining community sports leagues in most jurisdictions.", "They consistently score lower than peers on standardized tests due to social isolation."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what tension underlies the variation in regulatory approaches to homeschooling across jurisdictions?", options: ["The tension between homeschooling advocacy organizations and standardized testing companies over funding.", "The tension between religious homeschooling families and non-religious homeschooling families over curriculum content.", "The tension between rural and urban school districts over the allocation of teaching resources.", "The tension between a parent's authority over their child's upbringing and the state's interest in ensuring adequate education for civic participation."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT presented as a reason families choose to homeschool their children?", options: ["A desire to tailor instructional pace to a child's individual needs", "The conclusion that public schools fail to adequately prepare students for the modern economy", "Concerns about school safety", "A guarantee that homeschooling will result in higher college acceptance rates than conventional schooling"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"disentangle\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["separate", "fund", "confirm", "ignore"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"countervailing\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["overwhelming", "regulatory", "supporting", "counterbalancing"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"This selection effect\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The process by which children are selected for standardized testing", "The tendency of regulatory agencies to selectively enforce homeschooling laws", "The fact that families who choose to homeschool and participate in research tend to have more resources and education than the general population", "The decline in homeschooling rates after the pandemic ended"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view on whether increased regulation of homeschooling is justified?", options: ["The author concludes that homeschooling neglect cases are too rare to warrant any policy discussion.", "The author suggests that the appropriate level of regulation cannot be settled by empirical evidence alone because it depends on how one weighs competing values of parental authority and child welfare.", "The author argues that homeschooling advocacy organizations have exaggerated the risk of overregulation for political reasons.", "The author believes documented cases of neglect justify uniform mandatory testing requirements in every jurisdiction."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the structure and purpose of the author's argument in this passage?", options: ["The author dismisses concerns about educational neglect as unfounded by the end of the passage.", "The author primarily uses personal anecdotes about specific homeschooling families to support broad policy conclusions.", "The author builds a single, escalating argument in favor of expanding homeschooling regulation, paragraph by paragraph.", "The author presents competing claims and evidence on homeschooling's outcomes and regulation, repeatedly qualifying each claim rather than endorsing one side."], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
